---
import KnowledgeArticle from '../../../components/knowledge/KnowledgeArticle.astro';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the difference between journey mapping and journey analytics?', a: 'Journey mapping is a design exercise: a team describes the steps and emotions a customer goes through, often from research and workshops. Journey analytics measures the journeys customers actually take, from event data, at scale.' },
  { q: 'Is customer journey analytics the same as web or product analytics?', a: 'Product and web analytics focus on behaviour inside one digital channel. Journey analytics follows the customer across channels and across the operational steps behind them, such as verification, delivery, billing and support.' },
  { q: 'What data do you need for customer journey analytics?', a: 'Timestamped events from each channel and system the customer touches, plus a way to recognise that events in different systems belong to the same customer or order. That second part, identity resolution, is usually the hardest.' },
  { q: 'Does journey analytics prove why customers drop out?', a: 'It shows where they drop out and which segments are affected. Explaining why needs further evidence: what changed at that step, whether the drop is consistent across segments and whether reversing the change restores completion.' },
];
---
<KnowledgeArticle
  title="What is customer journey analytics?"
  metaTitle="What Is Customer Journey Analytics? Guide"
